      "problem": "Let $a=-53$ and $n=7.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $r=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-53$ by $7$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-8<-53÷7<-7.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-8.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-53-(-8)(7)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-53=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-53={{(-8)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 7+{{3}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r=3.$",
